window.DOCS["6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3"]={"id":"6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3","summary":{"title_zh":"關於 2030 年前落實《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條款之工作計畫任務 1.1 與 1.2 指引要素建議案","what":"這是第一工作小組主席提出的決議草案，旨在提出一套自願性指引，以強化法律與政策框架，承認原住民族與地方社區之角色及其傳統領地，並將其納入空間規劃、生態系復育、以區域為基礎之保育及環境影響評估。","points":["鼓勵締約方，並邀請其他政府與相關行動者，依國家法律與國際法，將此指引或其要素納入空間規劃流程、國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫（NBSAPs）及環境影響評估程序，並尊重原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的婦女與青年）之權利（第 2 段）。","鼓勵締約方支持指引之傳播，包括將其翻譯成原住民族與地方社區之語言，並邀請其他政府與相關行動者依國家優先事項與情況提供支持（第 3 段）。","鼓勵締約方識別並推廣確保原住民族與地方社區土地權屬與治理之最佳實務，符合任務 1.2 及國家法律（第 4 段）。","在資源允許下，要求執行秘書邀請締約方、原住民族與地方社區及利害關係人分享其對承認傳統領地之觀點與經驗，彙整為綜合資訊報告，提交第 8(j) 條附屬機構與 SBSTTA，供其在目標 3 脈絡下未來審議（第 6 段）。","鼓勵締約方監測與評估指引之使用及成效，並於國家報告（包括透過資訊交換機制）中報告；社區監測系統與傳統知識系統可視為相關資料來源（第 8 段）。","指引建議應提供充足、永續且可及的財務與技術支持，以協助原住民族與地方社區落實土地權益保障與自主治理（附件第 33、50 段，附件全文仍在括號內，尚未議定）。"],"open":["是否通過（採納）此自願性指引（決議第 1 段「Adopts」尚在括號內，尚未議定）","附件第 8 至 15 段指導原則（精神與文化關係、習慣土地與水域權屬、人權基礎取徑、自由、事先與知情同意、傳統知識、性別與代際公平、多元價值體系）是否列入","附件第 26 段：傳統領地是否應列為報告內容，或僅應「考慮納入報告」（括號用語）"]},"summaryReviewed":true,"pages":[{"p":"p001","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p001.md","en":"                                                                                              CBD/COP/17/L.28\n                                                                                   Distr.: Limited\n                                                                                   30 October 2026\n                                                                                   Original: English\n\n\n\n\nConference of the Parties to the\nConvention on Biological Diversity\nSeventeenth meeting\nYerevan, 19–30 October 2026\nAgenda item 10 (b) (i)\nImplementation of Article 8(j) and other provisions of the\nConvention on Biological Diversity related to indigenous\npeoples and local communities: guidelines to strengthen the\nlegal and policy framework for the implementation of\nTargets 2 and 3 of the Kunming-Montreal Global\nBiodiversity Framework, including on indigenous and\ntraditional territories, to support the protection and\nrestoration practices led by indigenous peoples and local\ncommunities (Task 1.1); and guidelines for the inclusion and\nconsideration of traditional lands and of resource use in\nspatial planning processes and environmental impact\nassessments (Task 1.2)\n\n       Proposed elements for the guidelines related to Tasks 1.1 and 1.2 of the\n       programme of work on Article 8(j) and other provisions of the\n       Convention on Biological Diversity related to indigenous peoples and\n       local communities to 2030\n       Draft decision submitted by the Chair of Working Group I\n\n               The Conference of the Parties,\n             Recalling its decision 16/4 of 30 October 2024, by which it adopted the programme of work\n       on Article 8(j) and other provisions of the Convention on Biological Diversity1 related to indigenous\n       peoples and local communities to 2030, and in particular Tasks 1.1 and 1.2, directed to the Subsidiary\n       Body on Article 8(j) and Other Provisions of the Convention on Biological Diversity Related to\n       Indigenous Peoples and Local Communities, to develop guidelines to strengthen the legal and policy\n       framework for the implementation of Targets 2 and 3 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity\n       Framework,2 including on indigenous and traditional territories, and guidelines for the inclusion and\n       consideration of traditional lands and of resource use in spatial planning processes and environmental\n       impact assessments to support the implementation of Targets 1 and 14 of the Framework,\n       respectively, and recognizing the alignment of the programme of work with the Framework,\n             Recalling also decision 16/6 of 1 November 2024 on the role of people of African descent,\n       comprising collectives embodying traditional lifestyles, in the implementation of the Convention on\n       Biological Diversity,\n\n\n1 United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1760, No. 30619.\n2 Decision 15/4, annex.","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28\n發行：限閱\n2026年10月30日\n原文：英文","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28<br />\n發行：限閱<br />\n2026年10月30日<br />\n原文：英文</p>"},{"md":"生物多樣性公約\n締約方大會\n第十七次會議\n葉里溫，2026年10月19日至30日\n議程項目 10 (b) (i)\n關於執行第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款：旨在強化執行《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》目標 2 與目標 3 之法律與政策架構指南，包括關於原住民與傳統領地之內容，以支持由原住民族與地方社區主導之保護與復育實務（任務 1.1）；以及關於在空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中納入並考量傳統土地與資源利用之指南（任務 1.2）","html":"<p>生物多樣性公約<br />\n締約方大會<br />\n第十七次會議<br />\n葉里溫，2026年10月19日至30日<br />\n議程項目 10 (b) (i)<br />\n關於執行第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款：旨在強化執行《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》目標 2 與目標 3 之法律與政策架構指南，包括關於原住民與傳統領地之內容，以支持由原住民族與地方社區主導之保護與復育實務（任務 1.1）；以及關於在空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中納入並考量傳統土地與資源利用之指南（任務 1.2）</p>"},{"md":"關於 2030 年前針對第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款工作計畫之任務 1.1 與 1.2 相關指南之擬議要素\n第一工作小組主席提交之決議草案\n締約方大會，\n**回顧**其 2024 年 10 月 30 日之決議 16/4，該決議通過了 2030 年前針對第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》<sup>1</sup>中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款之工作計畫，特別是任務 1.1 與 1.2，並指示關於第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款之科學、技術及工藝諮詢附屬機構，制定旨在強化執行《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》<sup>2</sup>目標 2 與目標 3 之法律與政策架構指南（包括關於原住民與傳統領地之內容），以及旨在支持執行該綱要目標 1 與目標 14 之指南，分別關於在空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中納入並考量傳統土地與資源利用，並認可該工作計畫與綱要之一致性，\n**亦回顧** 2024 年 11 月 1 日關於非洲裔人民（包含體現傳統生活方式之集體）在執行《生物多樣性公約》中所扮演角色之決議 16/6，","html":"<p>關於 2030 年前針對第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款工作計畫之任務 1.1 與 1.2 相關指南之擬議要素<br />\n第一工作小組主席提交之決議草案<br />\n締約方大會，<br />\n<strong>回顧</strong>其 2024 年 10 月 30 日之決議 16/4，該決議通過了 2030 年前針對第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》<sup>1</sup>中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款之工作計畫，特別是任務 1.1 與 1.2，並指示關於第八條 (j) 項及《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之其他條款之科學、技術及工藝諮詢附屬機構，制定旨在強化執行《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》<sup>2</sup>目標 2 與目標 3 之法律與政策架構指南（包括關於原住民與傳統領地之內容），以及旨在支持執行該綱要目標 1 與目標 14 之指南，分別關於在空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中納入並考量傳統土地與資源利用，並認可該工作計畫與綱要之一致性，<br />\n<strong>亦回顧</strong> 2024 年 11 月 1 日關於非洲裔人民（包含體現傳統生活方式之集體）在執行《生物多樣性公約》中所扮演角色之決議 16/6，</p>"},{"md":"---\n<sup>1</sup> 聯合國，條約集，第 1760 卷，第 30619 號。\n<sup>2</sup> 決議 15/4，附件。","html":"<hr />\n<p><sup>1</sup> 聯合國，條約集，第 1760 卷，第 30619 號。<br />\n<sup>2</sup> 決議 15/4，附件。</p>"}]},{"p":"p002","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p002.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n              Acknowledging the need to address the underrepresentation of indigenous peoples and local\n       communities from various regions and specific challenges faced by those from developing countries\n       in the work of the Convention,\n             Taking note of the output of the expert workshop to support the implementation of Tasks 1.1\n       and 1.2 of the programme of work on Article 8(j) and other provisions of the Convention related to\n       indigenous peoples and local communities, including draft guidelines,3\n              1.    [Adopts] the voluntary guidelines to strengthen the legal and policy framework for the\n       recognition of the role of indigenous peoples and local communities and their indigenous and\n       traditional territories in spatial planning, ecosystem restoration, area-based conservation and\n       environmental impact assessments contained in the annex to the present decision;\n             2.     Encourages Parties, and invites other Governments, to integrate and apply the\n       guidelines or their elements, as appropriate and in accordance with national legal frameworks and\n       relevant international law, in spatial planning processes, national biodiversity strategies and action\n       plans and environmental impact assessment procedures, respecting the rights of indigenous peoples\n       and local communities, including women and youth among them;\n              3.     Also encourages Parties, and invites other Governments and relevant actors in a position\n       to do so, to support the dissemination of the guidelines, including through their translation into the\n       languages of indigenous peoples and local communities, in accordance with national priorities and\n       circumstances;\n              4.     Further encourages Parties to identify and promote best practices to secure land tenure\n       and governance by indigenous peoples and local communities, in line with Task 1.2 of the\n       programme of work on Article 8(j) and other provisions of the Convention on Biological Diversity\n       related to indigenous peoples and local communities and in accordance with national legislation and\n       circumstances and international obligations;\n             5.     Invites Parties and other Governments, in partnership with indigenous peoples and local\n       communities, women, youth and relevant stakeholders, to identify and share their technical\n       cooperation and capacity-building and development needs for implementing the voluntary guidelines\n       through the clearing-house mechanism of the Convention, and invites Parties, other Governments,\n       regional and subregional technical and scientific cooperation support centres and relevant\n       organizations to offer support to address the capacity needs thus identified;\n              6.     Requests the Executive Secretary, subject to the availability of resources, to invite\n       Parties, indigenous peoples and local communities, and relevant stakeholders to share their views\n       and experiences on the recognition of indigenous and traditional territories, where applicable, in\n       accordance with national circumstances and legislation, including national approaches to the\n       governance, designation, classification and reporting of such territories, and compile the information\n       received to provide a consolidated informative report to the Subsidiary Body on Article 8(j) and\n       Other Provisions of the Convention on Biological Diversity Related to Indigenous Peoples and Local\n       Communities and the Subsidiary Body on Scientific, Technical and Technological Advice, with a\n       view to potential consideration by those bodies in their further work in the context of Target 3 of the\n       Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework and its monitoring framework;\n              7.     Requests the Global Coordination Entity, subject to the availability of resources, and\n       invites the regional and subregional technical and scientific cooperation support centres, to facilitate\n       capacity-building, technical cooperation and the exchange of information to assist interested Parties,\n       indigenous peoples and local communities and other relevant stakeholders in implementing the\n       voluntary guidelines;\n             8.     Encourages Parties, indigenous peoples and local communities and relevant\n       stakeholders, as appropriate, to monitor and evaluate the use and effectiveness of the guidelines and\n\n3 CBD/A8J/WS/2025/2/3.\n\n\n\n2/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"**承認**在公約工作中，有必要解決來自不同區域的原住民族與地方社區代表性不足的問題，以及開發中國家所面臨的特定挑戰，","html":"<p><strong>承認</strong>在公約工作中，有必要解決來自不同區域的原住民族與地方社區代表性不足的問題，以及開發中國家所面臨的特定挑戰，</p>"},{"md":"**注意到**旨在支持執行第八條(j)工作計畫任務 1.1 與 1.2，以及公約中其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條款之專家工作坊成果，包括決議草案，³","html":"<p><strong>注意到</strong>旨在支持執行第八條(j)工作計畫任務 1.1 與 1.2，以及公約中其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條款之專家工作坊成果，包括決議草案，³</p>"},{"md":"1. [採納] 本決議附件所載之自願性指南，以強化在空間規劃、生態系復育、以區域為基礎的保育及環境影響評估中，對於承認原住民族與地方社區及其原住民族與傳統領地之法律與政策框架；","html":"<p>1. [採納] 本決議附件所載之自願性指南，以強化在空間規劃、生態系復育、以區域為基礎的保育及環境影響評估中，對於承認原住民族與地方社區及其原住民族與傳統領地之法律與政策框架；</p>"},{"md":"2. **鼓勵**締約方，並**邀請**其他政府，在尊重原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）權利的前提下，視情況並根據國家法律框架及相關國際法，將該指南或其要素整合並應用於空間規劃程序、國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫以及環境影響評估程序中；","html":"<p>2. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，並<strong>邀請</strong>其他政府，在尊重原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）權利的前提下，視情況並根據國家法律框架及相關國際法，將該指南或其要素整合並應用於空間規劃程序、國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫以及環境影響評估程序中；</p>"},{"md":"3. 亦**鼓勵**締約方，並**邀請**其他政府及有能力之相關利害關係人，根據國家優先事項與情況，支持該指南之傳播，包括透過將其翻譯成原住民族與地方社區之語言；","html":"<p>3. 亦<strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，並<strong>邀請</strong>其他政府及有能力之相關利害關係人，根據國家優先事項與情況，支持該指南之傳播，包括透過將其翻譯成原住民族與地方社區之語言；</p>"},{"md":"4. 進一步**鼓勵**締約方，根據第八條(j)工作計畫之任務 1.2，以及生物多樣性公約中其他與原住民族與地方社區相關之條款，並符合國家立法、情況及國際義務，識別並推廣確保原住民族與地方社區土地權屬與治理之最佳實務；","html":"<p>4. 進一步<strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，根據第八條(j)工作計畫之任務 1.2，以及生物多樣性公約中其他與原住民族與地方社區相關之條款，並符合國家立法、情況及國際義務，識別並推廣確保原住民族與地方社區土地權屬與治理之最佳實務；</p>"},{"md":"5. **邀請**締約方與其他政府，與原住民族與地方社區、女性、青年及相關利害關係人合作，透過公約之資訊交換機制，識別並分享其為執行自願性指南所需之技術合作、能力建構與發展需求；並**邀請**締約方、其他政府、區域與次區域技術及科學合作支援中心以及相關組織，提供支援以應對由此識別之能力需求；","html":"<p>5. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方與其他政府，與原住民族與地方社區、女性、青年及相關利害關係人合作，透過公約之資訊交換機制，識別並分享其為執行自願性指南所需之技術合作、能力建構與發展需求；並<strong>邀請</strong>締約方、其他政府、區域與次區域技術及科學合作支援中心以及相關組織，提供支援以應對由此識別之能力需求；</p>"},{"md":"6. **請求**執行秘書在資源可及的情況下，**邀請**締約方、原住民族與地方社區及相關利害關係人，在符合國家情況與立法之範圍內，分享其對於（如適用時）承認原住民族與傳統領地之看法與經驗，包括對於此類領地之治理、指定、分類與報告之國家方法，並彙編所收到的資訊，以向「關於生物多樣性公約第八條(j)及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條款之附屬機構」以及「科學、技術及工藝諮詢附屬機構」提供一份綜合性資訊報告，以供上述機構在昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要及其監測架構之目標 3 背景下，於其後續工作中進行潛在審議；","html":"<p>6. <strong>請求</strong>執行秘書在資源可及的情況下，<strong>邀請</strong>締約方、原住民族與地方社區及相關利害關係人，在符合國家情況與立法之範圍內，分享其對於（如適用時）承認原住民族與傳統領地之看法與經驗，包括對於此類領地之治理、指定、分類與報告之國家方法，並彙編所收到的資訊，以向「關於生物多樣性公約第八條(j)及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條款之附屬機構」以及「科學、技術及工藝諮詢附屬機構」提供一份綜合性資訊報告，以供上述機構在昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要及其監測架構之目標 3 背景下，於其後續工作中進行潛在審議；</p>"},{"md":"7. **請求**全球協調實體在資源可及的情況下，並**邀請**區域與次區域技術及科學合作支援中心，促進能力建構、技術合作與資訊交換，以協助感興趣的締約方、原住民族與地方社區及其他相關利害關係人執行該自願性指南；","html":"<p>7. <strong>請求</strong>全球協調實體在資源可及的情況下，並<strong>邀請</strong>區域與次區域技術及科學合作支援中心，促進能力建構、技術合作與資訊交換，以協助感興趣的締約方、原住民族與地方社區及其他相關利害關係人執行該自願性指南；</p>"},{"md":"8. **鼓勵**締約方、原住民族與地方社區及相關利害關係人，視情況監測並評估該指南的使用情形與成效。","html":"<p>8. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方、原住民族與地方社區及相關利害關係人，視情況監測並評估該指南的使用情形與成效。</p>"},{"md":"***","html":"<hr />"},{"md":"³ CBD/A8J/WS/2025/2/3.","html":"<p>³ CBD/A8J/WS/2025/2/3.</p>"}]},{"p":"p003","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p003.md","en":"                                                                                              CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n          report thereon in national reports, including through the clearing-house mechanism, and by means of\n          other appropriate mechanisms, such as community-based monitoring and information systems,\n          which should be recognized as relevant data sources, in accordance with national circumstances;\n                 9.     Encourages Parties to strengthen the effective participation of indigenous peoples and\n          local communities and, where applicable, people of African descent, comprising collectives\n          embodying traditional lifestyles, in the design, implementation and monitoring of biodiversity\n          policies and programmes, as well as in biodiversity assessments and reporting, ensuring their full and\n          effective participation and incorporating community-based monitoring indicators and traditional\n          knowledge systems as complementary to other data sources, in accordance with national legislation,\n          priorities and circumstances.\n\n          Annex\n          Voluntary guidelines to strengthen the legal and policy framework for the recognition\n          of the role of indigenous peoples and local communities and their indigenous and\n          traditional territories in spatial planning, ecosystem restoration, area-based\n          conservation and environmental impact assessments\n\n          I.      Rationale\n          1.     By its decision 16/5 of 1 November 2024, the Conference of the Parties to the Convention on\n          Biological Diversity4 established the Subsidiary Body on Article 8(j) and Other Provisions of the\n          Convention on Biological Diversity Related to Indigenous Peoples and Local Communities. The\n          Subsidiary Body is mandated to provide advice to the Conference of the Parties, other subsidiary\n          bodies and, upon request, the Conference of the Parties serving as the meeting of the Parties to the\n          Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety5 and the Conference of the Parties serving as the meeting of the\n          Parties to the Nagoya Protocol on Access to Genetic Resources and the Fair and Equitable Sharing\n          of Benefits Arising from Their Utilization, 6 on matters concerning indigenous peoples and local\n          communities.\n          2.     By its decision 16/4, the Conference of the Parties adopted the programme of work on\n          Article 8(j) and other provisions of the Convention on Biological Diversity related to indigenous\n          peoples and local communities to 2030. The objective of the programme of work is to promote the\n          implementation of Article 8(j) and other provisions of the Convention related to indigenous peoples\n          and local communities, as well as of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework.7 In the\n          programme of work, the Conference of the Parties affirmed the importance of promoting the full and\n          effective participation of indigenous peoples and local communities at all stages and levels of\n          implementation, in recognition of the close connection that indigenous peoples and local\n          communities have with biological diversity and the Convention and its Protocols.\n          3.     Element 1 of the programme of work, on conservation and ecosystem restoration, includes two\n          tasks for the Subsidiary Body:\n                  Task 1.1. Develop guidelines,8 with the full and effective participation of indigenous peoples\n                  and local communities, to strengthen the legal and policy framework for the implementation\n                  of Targets 2 and 3 of the Framework, including on indigenous and traditional territories, to\n\n\n\n\n4 United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1760, No. 30619.\n5 Ibid., vol. 2226, No. 30619.\n6 Ibid., vol. 3008, No. 30619.\n7 Decision 15/4, annex.\n8\n    For the purpose of the present guidelines, “guidelines” refers to voluntary guidelines.\n\n                                                                                                           3/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"於國家報告中進行相關報告，包括透過資訊交換機制，以及透過其他適當機制，例如以社區為基礎的監測與資訊系統，且在符合國家情況下，應將其視為相關資料來源；","html":"<p>於國家報告中進行相關報告，包括透過資訊交換機制，以及透過其他適當機制，例如以社區為基礎的監測與資訊系統，且在符合國家情況下，應將其視為相關資料來源；</p>"},{"md":"9.\n**鼓勵**締約方加強原住民族與地方社區，以及在適用情況下，包含體現傳統生活方式之集體的非洲裔人民，在生物多樣性政策與計畫的設計、實施與監測，以及生物多樣性評估與報告中的有效參與，確保其充分且有效的參與，並將以社區為基礎的監測指標與傳統知識系統納入，作為其他資料來源之補充，並應符合國家立法、優先事項與情況。","html":"<p>9.<br />\n<strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方加強原住民族與地方社區，以及在適用情況下，包含體現傳統生活方式之集體的非洲裔人民，在生物多樣性政策與計畫的設計、實施與監測，以及生物多樣性評估與報告中的有效參與，確保其充分且有效的參與，並將以社區為基礎的監測指標與傳統知識系統納入，作為其他資料來源之補充，並應符合國家立法、優先事項與情況。</p>"},{"md":"附件\n旨在強化於空間規劃、生態系復育、以區域為基礎之保育及環境影響評估中，承認原住民族與地方社區及其原住民與傳統領地角色之法律與政策框架之自願性指南","html":"<p>附件<br />\n旨在強化於空間規劃、生態系復育、以區域為基礎之保育及環境影響評估中，承認原住民族與地方社區及其原住民與傳統領地角色之法律與政策框架之自願性指南</p>"},{"md":"I.\n理由","html":"<p>I.<br />\n理由</p>"},{"md":"1.\n透過 2024 年 11 月 1 日之決議 16/5，生物多樣性公約締約方大會⁴ 成立了關於《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條文之附屬機構。該附屬機構受託就涉及原住民族與地方社區之事項，向締約方大會、其他附屬機構，以及應要求向擔任卡塔赫納生物安全議定書締約方會議⁵之締約方大會，以及擔任取得遺傳資源及其利用所產生利益公平公正分享之名古屋議定書締約方會議⁶之締約方大會提供建議。","html":"<p>1.<br />\n透過 2024 年 11 月 1 日之決議 16/5，生物多樣性公約締約方大會⁴ 成立了關於《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條文之附屬機構。該附屬機構受託就涉及原住民族與地方社區之事項，向締約方大會、其他附屬機構，以及應要求向擔任卡塔赫納生物安全議定書締約方會議⁵之締約方大會，以及擔任取得遺傳資源及其利用所產生利益公平公正分享之名古屋議定書締約方會議⁶之締約方大會提供建議。</p>"},{"md":"2.\n透過決議 16/4，締約方大會通過了關於 2030 年前《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之第 8(j) 條及其他條文的工作計畫。該工作計畫之目標在於促進《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之第 8(j) 條及其他條文，以及《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》之實施。⁷ 在該工作計畫中，締約方大會肯定了在實施的所有階段與層級促進原住民族與地方社區充分且有效參與的重要性，並承認原住民族與地方社區與生物多樣性、本公約及其議定書之間存在著緊密聯繫。","html":"<p>2.<br />\n透過決議 16/4，締約方大會通過了關於 2030 年前《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之第 8(j) 條及其他條文的工作計畫。該工作計畫之目標在於促進《生物多樣性公約》中與原住民族與地方社區相關之第 8(j) 條及其他條文，以及《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》之實施。⁷ 在該工作計畫中，締約方大會肯定了在實施的所有階段與層級促進原住民族與地方社區充分且有效參與的重要性，並承認原住民族與地方社區與生物多樣性、本公約及其議定書之間存在著緊密聯繫。</p>"},{"md":"3.\n工作計畫之要素 1（關於保育與生態系復育）包含兩項附屬機構之任務：\n任務 1.1. 在原住民族與地方社區充分且有效參與下，制定指南，⁸ 以強化實施綱要目標 2 與目標 3 之法律與政策框架，包括關於原住民與傳統領地之事項，以","html":"<p>3.<br />\n工作計畫之要素 1（關於保育與生態系復育）包含兩項附屬機構之任務：<br />\n任務 1.1. 在原住民族與地方社區充分且有效參與下，制定指南，⁸ 以強化實施綱要目標 2 與目標 3 之法律與政策框架，包括關於原住民與傳統領地之事項，以</p>"},{"md":"---\n⁴ 聯合國，條約集，第 1760 卷，第 30619 號。\n⁵ 同上，第 2226 卷，第 30619 號。\n⁶ 同上，第 3008 卷，第 30619 號。\n⁷ 決議 15/4，附件。\n⁸ 就本指南而言，「指南」係指自願性指南。","html":"<hr />\n<p>⁴ 聯合國，條約集，第 1760 卷，第 30619 號。<br />\n⁵ 同上，第 2226 卷，第 30619 號。<br />\n⁶ 同上，第 3008 卷，第 30619 號。<br />\n⁷ 決議 15/4，附件。<br />\n⁸ 就本指南而言，「指南」係指自願性指南。</p>"}]},{"p":"p004","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p004.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n             support the protection and restoration practices led by indigenous peoples and local\n             communities.\n             Task 1.2. Identify and promote best practices to secure land tenure and governance by\n             indigenous peoples and local communities and develop guidelines for the inclusion and\n             consideration of traditional lands and of resource use in spatial planning processes and\n             environmental impact assessments, in accordance with national legislation and international\n             obligations.\n       4.     The present guidelines have been developed by the Subsidiary Body on Article 8(j) and Other\n       Provisions of the Convention on Biological Diversity Related to Indigenous Peoples and Local\n       Communities pursuant to Tasks 1.1 and 1.2 of the programme of work on Article 8(j) and other\n       provisions of the Convention related to indigenous peoples and local communities. The guidelines\n       are aimed at Parties, indigenous peoples and local communities and other relevant actors, including,\n       relevant conservation and ecosystem restoration organizations, academia and research institutions,\n       the private sector and other entities that will implement all elements of the guidelines.\n       5.    While applying these guidelines, Parties should, as appropriate, adopt a comprehensive\n       protection and a differentiated approach for indigenous peoples in voluntary isolation and initial\n       contact, in accordance with national circumstances, with a view to strengthening the legal and policy\n       framework for spatial planning, ecosystem restoration and conservation, and to avoiding activities\n       that may put them at risk.\n\n       II.   Objectives\n       6.    The objectives of the guidelines are to:\n              (a) Provide guidance on the inclusion and consideration of traditional lands, territories and\n       waters, and resource use systems of indigenous peoples and local communities in spatial planning\n       processes and environmental impact assessments to support the implementation of Targets 1 and 14\n       of the Framework;\n             (b) Provide legal and policy guidance to support the full and effective participation of\n       indigenous peoples and local communities in decision-making processes and the implementation of\n       ecosystem restoration activities, recognizing their contribution to the implementation of Target 2 of\n       the Framework through their traditional knowledge, innovations and practices and through\n       indigenous and traditional governance systems;\n             (c) Provide legal and policy guidance for the identification, recognition, protection and\n       promotion of, and respect and support for, the indigenous and traditional territories of indigenous\n       peoples and local communities to support the implementation of Target 3 of the Framework.\n\n       III. Guiding principles\n       7.     The present voluntary guidelines should be applied in accordance with national legislation and\n       circumstances and international law and instruments and in a manner that is consistent with section C\n       of the Framework and the general principles outlined in the programme of work.\n       [A.   Spiritual and cultural relationships\n       8.     Conservation and ecosystem restoration measures, as well as spatial planning and effective\n       management processes addressing land- and sea-use change and environmental impact assessments,\n       should be designed in a manner that recognizes and respects the spiritual and biocultural relationships\n       that indigenous peoples and local communities have with biodiversity in their lands, territories and\n       waters.\n\n\n\n\n4/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"支持由原住民族與地方社區主導的保護與復育實務。\n任務 1.2. 識別並推廣由原住民族與地方社區確保土地權屬與治理的最佳實務，並制定指南，以在符合國家立法與國際義務的情況下，將傳統土地及資源利用納入空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中並予以考量。","html":"<p>支持由原住民族與地方社區主導的保護與復育實務。<br />\n任務 1.2. 識別並推廣由原住民族與地方社區確保土地權屬與治理的最佳實務，並制定指南，以在符合國家立法與國際義務的情況下，將傳統土地及資源利用納入空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中並予以考量。</p>"},{"md":"4.\n本指南係根據關於《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條文之工作計畫之任務 1.1 與 1.2，由關於《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條文之附屬機構所制定。本指南之對象為締約方、原住民族與地方社區及其他相關參與者，包括相關的保育與生態系復育組織、學術界與研究機構、私營部門以及將執行本指南所有要素之其他實體。","html":"<p>4.<br />\n本指南係根據關於《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條文之工作計畫之任務 1.1 與 1.2，由關於《生物多樣性公約》第 8(j) 條及其他與原住民族與地方社區相關條文之附屬機構所制定。本指南之對象為締約方、原住民族與地方社區及其他相關參與者，包括相關的保育與生態系復育組織、學術界與研究機構、私營部門以及將執行本指南所有要素之其他實體。</p>"},{"md":"5.\n在應用本指南時，締約方應視情況根據國情，針對處於自願隔離與初始接觸狀態之原住民族，採取全面保護與差異化之方法，以強化空間規劃、生態系復育與保育之法律與政策框架，並避免可能使其面臨風險之活動。","html":"<p>5.<br />\n在應用本指南時，締約方應視情況根據國情，針對處於自願隔離與初始接觸狀態之原住民族，採取全面保護與差異化之方法，以強化空間規劃、生態系復育與保育之法律與政策框架，並避免可能使其面臨風險之活動。</p>"},{"md":"II.","html":"<p>II.</p>","pre":"框架，並避免可能使其面臨風險之活動。\n\n"},{"md":"目標","html":"<p>目標</p>","pre":"免可能使其面臨風險之活動。\n\nII.\n\n"},{"md":"6.","html":"<p>6.</p>"},{"md":"本指南之目標為：","html":"<p>本指南之目標為：</p>"},{"md":"(a) 就將原住民族與地方社區之傳統土地、領土與水域，以及資源利用系統納入空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中並予以考量，提供指引，以支持《綱要》目標 1 與目標 14 之實施；\n(b) 提供法律與政策指引，以支持原住民族與地方社區充分且有效地參與決策程序及生態系復育活動之實施；並承認其透過傳統知識、創新與實務，以及透過原住民族與傳統治理系統，對《綱要》目標 2 之實施所做出的貢獻；\n(c) 為識別、承認、保護與推廣，以及尊重與支持原住民族與地方社區之原住民族與傳統領土，提供法律與政策指引，以支持《綱要》目標 3 之實施。","html":"<p>(a) 就將原住民族與地方社區之傳統土地、領土與水域，以及資源利用系統納入空間規劃程序與環境影響評估中並予以考量，提供指引，以支持《綱要》目標 1 與目標 14 之實施；<br />\n(b) 提供法律與政策指引，以支持原住民族與地方社區充分且有效地參與決策程序及生態系復育活動之實施；並承認其透過傳統知識、創新與實務，以及透過原住民族與傳統治理系統，對《綱要》目標 2 之實施所做出的貢獻；<br />\n(c) 為識別、承認、保護與推廣，以及尊重與支持原住民族與地方社區之原住民族與傳統領土，提供法律與政策指引，以支持《綱要》目標 3 之實施。</p>"},{"md":"III. 指導原則","html":"<p>III. 指導原則</p>"},{"md":"7.\n本自願性指南應根據國家立法與國情，以及國際法與法律文書，並以與《綱要》C 節及工作計畫中所概述之一般原則一致之方式進行應用。","html":"<p>7.<br />\n本自願性指南應根據國家立法與國情，以及國際法與法律文書，並以與《綱要》C 節及工作計畫中所概述之一般原則一致之方式進行應用。</p>"},{"md":"[A.","html":"<p>[A.</p>"},{"md":"精神與文化關係","html":"<p>精神與文化關係</p>"},{"md":"8.\n保育與生態系復育措施，以及處理陸域與海域利用變遷之空間規劃與有效管理程序，以及環境影響評估，其設計應能承認並尊重原住民族與地方社區在其土地、領土與水域中，與生物多樣性之間所存在之精神與生物文化關係。","html":"<p>8.<br />\n保育與生態系復育措施，以及處理陸域與海域利用變遷之空間規劃與有效管理程序，以及環境影響評估，其設計應能承認並尊重原住民族與地方社區在其土地、領土與水域中，與生物多樣性之間所存在之精神與生物文化關係。</p>"}]},{"p":"p005","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p005.md","en":"                                                                                                               CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n        B.      Recognition of and respect for customary land\n                and water tenure and governance systems\n        9.    The customary tenure over land, water and territories, governance systems, customary law and\n        procedures of indigenous peoples and local communities should be recognized and respected.\n        C.      Human rights-based approach\n        10. Conservation and ecosystem restoration efforts, spatial planning and environmental impact\n        assessments should follow a human rights-based approach respecting, protecting and promoting the\n        rights of indigenous peoples and local communities, including through their full and effective\n        participation in decision-making, in line with section C of the Framework and in accordance with\n        relevant national legislation and circumstances, international instruments, including the United\n        Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples,9 and human rights law.\n        D.      Free, prior and informed consent\n        11. The free, prior and informed consent10 of indigenous peoples and local communities should be\n        respected in the design and implementation of spatial planning and environmental impact assessment\n        activities, as appropriate, including those related to the designation and management of conservation\n        and restoration areas that may have an impact on their lands, territories, waters, resources and cultural\n        heritage, in accordance with national circumstances.\n        E.      Traditional knowledge, innovations and practices\n        12. The traditional knowledge, innovations and practices of indigenous peoples and local\n        communities provide valuable cultural guidance and information to enhance and support the\n        implementation of conservation and ecosystem restoration measures, spatial planning and effective\n        management processes addressing land- and sea-use change and environmental impact assessments,\n        in addition to other knowledge systems. Traditional knowledge systems should therefore be\n        recognized, respected, valued and protected, including through the respect for the free, prior and\n        informed consent of traditional knowledge holders.\n        F.      Gender equality and intergenerational equity\n        13. The critical roles of women and girls, children and youth, knowledge holders and elders from\n        indigenous peoples and local communities in conservation and ecosystem restoration efforts, spatial\n        planning and environmental impact assessments should be recognized. Women and girls among\n        indigenous peoples and local communities play an important role in the preservation and\n        transmission of traditional knowledge. Gender-responsive approaches should address barriers to the\n        participation, leadership and benefit-sharing of women and girls, and children and youth from\n        indigenous peoples and local communities and should safeguard the transmission of gender-specific\n        knowledge.\n        14. The implementation of the present guidelines should be guided by the principles of\n        intergenerational equity to ensure that the needs of the present generation are met without\n        compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs and that younger generations\n        can participate meaningfully in decision-making at all levels.\n        G.      Recognition of diverse value systems\n        15. Conservation and ecosystem restoration efforts, spatial planning and environmental impact\n        assessments should be undertaken in a manner that respects the diverse value systems of indigenous\n        peoples and local communities. Such concepts as Mother Earth, spirituality, nature’s gifts and the\n        rights of nature should be acknowledged and respected in accordance with section C of the\n        Framework and national circumstances. The cultural, spiritual and holistic relationships of\n\n9 General Assembly resolution 61/295, annex.\n10 All references to “free, prior and informed consent” refer to the tripartite terminology of “prior and informed consent”, “free,\n\nprior and informed consent” or “approval and involvement”.\n\n                                                                                                                                5/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"B.","html":"<p>B.</p>"},{"md":"對習慣性土地與水資源權屬及治理制度之承認與尊重","html":"<p>對習慣性土地與水資源權屬及治理制度之承認與尊重</p>"},{"md":"9.\n應承認並尊重原住民族與地方社區對土地、水資源及領土之習慣性權屬、治理制度、習慣法及程序。","html":"<p>9.<br />\n應承認並尊重原住民族與地方社區對土地、水資源及領土之習慣性權屬、治理制度、習慣法及程序。</p>"},{"md":"C.","html":"<p>C.</p>"},{"md":"以人權為基礎之方法","html":"<p>以人權為基礎之方法</p>","pre":"屬、治理制度、習慣法及程序。\n\nC.\n\n"},{"md":"10.\n保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估，應遵循以人權為基礎之方法，尊重、保護並促進原住民族與地方社區之權利，包括透過其全面且有效地參與決策，並應符合綱要之C節，且符合相關國家立法與情況、國際文書（包括《聯合國原住民族權利宣言》$^9$）及人權法。","html":"<p>10.<br />\n保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估，應遵循以人權為基礎之方法，尊重、保護並促進原住民族與地方社區之權利，包括透過其全面且有效地參與決策，並應符合綱要之C節，且符合相關國家立法與情況、國際文書（包括《聯合國原住民族權利宣言》<sup>9</sup>）及人權法。</p>"},{"md":"D.","html":"<p>D.</p>"},{"md":"自由、事先及知情同意","html":"<p>自由、事先及知情同意</p>","pre":"利宣言》$^9$）及人權法。\n\nD.\n\n"},{"md":"11.\n在空間規劃與環境影響評估活動之設計與執行過程中，應視情況尊重原住民族與地方社區之自由、事先及知情同意$^{10}$，包括與指定及管理可能對其土地、領土、水資源、資源及文化遺產產生影響之保育與復育區相關之活動，並應符合國家情況。","html":"<p>11.<br />\n在空間規劃與環境影響評估活動之設計與執行過程中，應視情況尊重原住民族與地方社區之自由、事先及知情同意<sup>10</sup>，包括與指定及管理可能對其土地、領土、水資源、資源及文化遺產產生影響之保育與復育區相關之活動，並應符合國家情況。</p>"},{"md":"E.","html":"<p>E.</p>"},{"md":"傳統知識、創新與實踐","html":"<p>傳統知識、創新與實踐</p>","pre":"關之活動，並應符合國家情況。\n\nE.\n\n"},{"md":"12.\n除其他知識系統外，原住民族與地方社區之傳統知識、創新與實踐，能提供寶貴的文化指引與資訊，以增進並支持保育與生態系復育措施、空間規劃，以及應對陸域與海域利用變遷及環境影響評估之有效管理程序之執行。因此，傳統知識系統應受到承認、尊重、重視與保護，包括透過尊重傳統知識持有者之自由、事先及知情同意。","html":"<p>12.<br />\n除其他知識系統外，原住民族與地方社區之傳統知識、創新與實踐，能提供寶貴的文化指引與資訊，以增進並支持保育與生態系復育措施、空間規劃，以及應對陸域與海域利用變遷及環境影響評估之有效管理程序之執行。因此，傳統知識系統應受到承認、尊重、重視與保護，包括透過尊重傳統知識持有者之自由、事先及知情同意。</p>"},{"md":"F.","html":"<p>F.</p>"},{"md":"性別平等與世代公平","html":"<p>性別平等與世代公平</p>"},{"md":"13.\n應承認原住民族與地方社區之女性與女孩、兒童與青年、知識持有者及長者，在保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估中所扮演之關鍵角色。原住民族與地方社區之女性與女孩，在傳統知識的保存與傳承中扮演重要角色。具性別回應之方法，應解決原住民族與地方社區之女性與女孩、兒童與青年在參與、領導及利益分享方面所面臨的障礙，並應保障性別特定知識之傳承。\n14.\n本指南之執行應以世代公平原則為指引，以確保在滿足當代需求之同時，不損及後代滿足其自身需求之能力，並確保年輕世代能有意義地參與各層級之決策。","html":"<p>13.<br />\n應承認原住民族與地方社區之女性與女孩、兒童與青年、知識持有者及長者，在保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估中所扮演之關鍵角色。原住民族與地方社區之女性與女孩，在傳統知識的保存與傳承中扮演重要角色。具性別回應之方法，應解決原住民族與地方社區之女性與女孩、兒童與青年在參與、領導及利益分享方面所面臨的障礙，並應保障性別特定知識之傳承。<br />\n14.<br />\n本指南之執行應以世代公平原則為指引，以確保在滿足當代需求之同時，不損及後代滿足其自身需求之能力，並確保年輕世代能有意義地參與各層級之決策。</p>"},{"md":"G.","html":"<p>G.</p>"},{"md":"對多元價值體系之承認","html":"<p>對多元價值體系之承認</p>"},{"md":"15.\n保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估，應以尊重原住民族與地方社區多元價值體系之方式進行。大地母親、精神性、自然的饋贈及自然權利等概念，應根據綱要之C節及國家情況予以認可與尊重。原住民族與地方社區之文化、精神及整體性關係……","html":"<p>15.<br />\n保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估，應以尊重原住民族與地方社區多元價值體系之方式進行。大地母親、精神性、自然的饋贈及自然權利等概念，應根據綱要之C節及國家情況予以認可與尊重。原住民族與地方社區之文化、精神及整體性關係……</p>"},{"md":"---\n$^9$ 聯合國大會決議 61/295，附件。\n$^{10}$ 所有提及「自由、事先及知情同意」之處，均指「事先及知情同意」、「自由、事先及知情同意」或「核准與參與」之三方術語。","html":"<hr />\n<p><sup>9</sup> 聯合國大會決議 61/295，附件。<br />\n<sup>10</sup> 所有提及「自由、事先及知情同意」之處，均指「事先及知情同意」、「自由、事先及知情同意」或「核准與參與」之三方術語。</p>"}]},{"p":"p006","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p006.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n        indigenous peoples and local communities with their lands, territories and waters and their distinct\n        knowledge systems and governance institutions should also be acknowledged and respected.]\n\n        IV. Inclusion and consideration of traditional lands\n            and of resource use in spatial planning processes\n        16. National laws and policy frameworks should, as appropriate, ensure the recognition of\n        traditional lands and resource use in participatory, integrated and biodiversity-inclusive spatial\n        planning and effective management processes that address land- and sea-use change, including those\n        led by or jointly developed with indigenous peoples and local communities in relation to their lands,\n        territories, waters and resources, including marine and coastal areas.\n        17. Spatial planning and effective management measures led by indigenous peoples and local\n        communities that address land- and sea-use change should be considered and reflected, as\n        appropriate, in the development, revision or updating of national biodiversity strategies and action\n        plans, as well as in land-use planning and marine spatial planning.\n        18. Spatial planning and land-use-related frameworks and processes should, as appropriate,\n        recognize land- and sea-use mapping led by indigenous peoples and local communities, developed\n        in accordance with their cultural, governance and spiritual dimensions and using traditional\n        toponyms.\n        19. Decision-making in spatial planning and effective management processes addressing land- and\n        sea-use change should promote cooperation and collaboration with indigenous peoples and local\n        communities, through the recognition, as appropriate, of indigenous and traditional governance\n        systems.\n\n        V.     Contributions to the effective restoration of degraded ecosystems11\n        20. The rights and practices of indigenous peoples and local communities in ecosystem restoration\n        activities, which are grounded in their holistic relationships with their lands, territories and waters\n        should be respected and promoted by, inter alia, supporting biocultural approaches, customary\n        sustainable use of biodiversity, traditional knowledge systems and other forms of stewardship, such\n        as those related to the governance and land tenure rights of indigenous peoples and local\n        communities.\n        21. Restoration efforts should, where applicable, take into account traditional knowledge and the\n        principles of free, prior and informed consent. In accordance with indigenous and traditional\n        governance systems, the roles of elders, knowledge holders, women and girls, and children and youth\n        from indigenous peoples and local communities should be recognized and strengthened in all aspects\n        of restoration.\n        22. Legislative, administrative and policy measures related to ecosystem restoration should be\n        developed in line with Target 2 of the Framework and in a manner that recognizes and safeguards\n        the contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities. Measures should be developed with\n        the full and effective participation of indigenous peoples and local communities at all stages and in\n        all processes related to planning, development, implementation and monitoring. Assessments of\n        degraded ecosystems should draw upon diverse knowledge systems, including scientific assessments\n        and the traditional knowledge systems of indigenous peoples and local communities.\n        23. Restoration efforts should be co-designed and co-developed with indigenous peoples and local\n        communities and relevant government authorities and reflected in national restoration plans and\n        national reporting, ensuring that the contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities are\n        visible, supported and tracked through the use of appropriate indicators.\n\n11 The territories, lands and waters of indigenous peoples and local communities should not be considered as inherently in need of\n\nrestoration.\n\n6/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"原住民族與地方社區及其土地、領域與水域，以及其獨特的知識系統與治理制度，亦應獲得承認與尊重。]","html":"<p>原住民族與地方社區及其土地、領域與水域，以及其獨特的知識系統與治理制度，亦應獲得承認與尊重。]</p>"},{"md":"IV. 於空間規劃程序中納入並考量傳統土地與資源利用","html":"<p>IV. 於空間規劃程序中納入並考量傳統土地與資源利用</p>"},{"md":"16. 國家法律與政策框架應在適當情況下，確保在參與式、整合性且具生物多樣性包容性的空間規劃與有效管理程序中，承認傳統土地與資源利用，並應處理陸域與海域利用之變遷，包括由原住民族與地方社區主導，或與其就其土地、領域、水域與資源（包括海洋與沿海區域）共同開發之程序。","html":"<p>16. 國家法律與政策框架應在適當情況下，確保在參與式、整合性且具生物多樣性包容性的空間規劃與有效管理程序中，承認傳統土地與資源利用，並應處理陸域與海域利用之變遷，包括由原住民族與地方社區主導，或與其就其土地、領域、水域與資源（包括海洋與沿海區域）共同開發之程序。</p>"},{"md":"17. 應在國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫的制定、修訂或更新，以及土地利用規劃與海洋空間規劃中，適當地考量並反映由原住民族與地方社區主導、且旨在處理陸域與海域利用變遷之空間規劃與有效管理措施。","html":"<p>17. 應在國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫的制定、修訂或更新，以及土地利用規劃與海洋空間規劃中，適當地考量並反映由原住民族與地方社區主導、且旨在處理陸域與海域利用變遷之空間規劃與有效管理措施。</p>"},{"md":"18. 空間規劃與土地利用相關之框架與程序，應在適當情況下，承認由原住民族與地方社區主導、並根據其文化、治理與精神維度及使用傳統地名所開發之陸域與海域利用地圖。","html":"<p>18. 空間規劃與土地利用相關之框架與程序，應在適當情況下，承認由原住民族與地方社區主導、並根據其文化、治理與精神維度及使用傳統地名所開發之陸域與海域利用地圖。</p>"},{"md":"19. 在處理陸域與海域利用變遷之空間規劃與有效管理程序的決策過程中，應透過適當地承認原住民與傳統治理制度，促進與原住民族及地方社區之合作與協作。","html":"<p>19. 在處理陸域與海域利用變遷之空間規劃與有效管理程序的決策過程中，應透過適當地承認原住民與傳統治理制度，促進與原住民族及地方社區之合作與協作。</p>"},{"md":"V.","html":"<p>V.</p>","pre":"進與原住民族及地方社區之合作與協作。\n\n"},{"md":"對退化生態系有效復育之貢獻$^{11}$","html":"<p>對退化生態系有效復育之貢獻<sup>11</sup></p>"},{"md":"20. 原住民族與地方社區在生態系復育活動中的權利與實踐，因其與土地、領域及水域之整體性關係，應受到尊重與促進，其手段應包括（但不限於）支持生物文化方法、生物多樣性之傳統永續利用、傳統知識系統及其他形式的管護，例如與原住民族與地方社區之治理及土地權屬權利相關之措施。","html":"<p>20. 原住民族與地方社區在生態系復育活動中的權利與實踐，因其與土地、領域及水域之整體性關係，應受到尊重與促進，其手段應包括（但不限於）支持生物文化方法、生物多樣性之傳統永續利用、傳統知識系統及其他形式的管護，例如與原住民族與地方社區之治理及土地權屬權利相關之措施。</p>"},{"md":"21. 復育工作在適用情況下，應考量傳統知識以及自由、事先及知情同意之原則。應根據原住民與傳統治理制度，在復育的所有面向中，承認並強化原住民族與地方社區之長者、知識持有者、女性與女孩，以及兒童與青年的角色。","html":"<p>21. 復育工作在適用情況下，應考量傳統知識以及自由、事先及知情同意之原則。應根據原住民與傳統治理制度，在復育的所有面向中，承認並強化原住民族與地方社區之長者、知識持有者、女性與女孩，以及兒童與青年的角色。</p>"},{"md":"22. 與生態系復育相關之立法、行政與政策措施，應與綱要之目標 2 一致進行制定，並以承認及保障原住民族與地方社區貢獻之方式進行。措施之制定，應確保原住民族與地方社區在與規劃、開發、執行及監測相關之所有階段與程序中，皆能充分且有效地參與。退化生態系之評估，應利用多元的知識系統，包括科學評估以及原住民族與地方社區之傳統知識系統。","html":"<p>22. 與生態系復育相關之立法、行政與政策措施，應與綱要之目標 2 一致進行制定，並以承認及保障原住民族與地方社區貢獻之方式進行。措施之制定，應確保原住民族與地方社區在與規劃、開發、執行及監測相關之所有階段與程序中，皆能充分且有效地參與。退化生態系之評估，應利用多元的知識系統，包括科學評估以及原住民族與地方社區之傳統知識系統。</p>"},{"md":"23. 復育工作應與原住民族與地方社區及相關政府機關共同設計與共同開發，並反映於國家復育計畫與國家報告中，以確保原住民族與地方社區之貢獻，能透過使用適當指標而變得可見、獲得支持並受到追蹤。","html":"<p>23. 復育工作應與原住民族與地方社區及相關政府機關共同設計與共同開發，並反映於國家復育計畫與國家報告中，以確保原住民族與地方社區之貢獻，能透過使用適當指標而變得可見、獲得支持並受到追蹤。</p>"},{"md":"$^{11}$ 原住民族與地方社區之領域、土地與水域，不應被視為本質上需要復育。","html":"<p><sup>11</sup> 原住民族與地方社區之領域、土地與水域，不應被視為本質上需要復育。</p>"}]},{"p":"p007","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p007.md","en":"                                                                                                         CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n       24. Restoration efforts in indigenous and traditional territories should, where possible, contribute\n       to food and water security, climate and community resilience, income generation and the cultural and\n       lifestyle strengthening of indigenous peoples and local communities.\n\n       VI. Recognition of and respect for indigenous and traditional territories in the\n           implementation of Target 3 of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity\n           Framework\n       25. Legal, administrative and policy measures for the national identification and recognition of,\n       and respect for, indigenous and traditional territories should be developed, adopted and implemented.\n       Those measures should recognize, as appropriate, the customary tenure systems, governance\n       institutions, cultural significance, sacred sites, conservation roles, traditional economies and rights\n       over lands, waters, territories and resources of indigenous peoples and local communities.\n       26. Indigenous and traditional territories, in many cases, contribute to the conservation and\n       sustainable use of biodiversity, as relevant to the implementation of Target 3 of the Framework.\n       Indigenous and traditional territories can also be complementary to and may be recognized separately\n       from protected areas and other effective area-based conservation measures, [and reported][and\n       considered for reporting12] as relevant to the implementation of Target 3 of the Framework, according\n       to the principle of free, prior and informed consent and to national circumstances, legislation and\n       frameworks. Where protected areas or other effective area-based conservation measures overlap\n       indigenous and traditional territories, management measures that ensure biodiversity outcomes\n       should apply, in accordance with national legislation and circumstances and the principle of free,\n       prior and informed consent, respecting the rights of indigenous peoples and local communities,\n       including over their traditional territories, to practice their customary sustainable use, management\n       and governance, as well as their cultural practices, according to national circumstances and\n       legislation.\n\n       VII. Environmental impact assessments\n       27. The Akwé: Kon Voluntary Guidelines for the Conduct of Cultural, Environmental and Social\n       Impact Assessments Regarding Developments Proposed to Take Place on, or Which Are Likely to\n       Impact on, Sacred Sites and on Lands and Waters Traditionally Occupied or Used by Indigenous and\n       Local Communities 13 provide a culturally appropriate framework for conducting environmental,\n       cultural and social impact assessments related to developments proposed on or near sacred sites, or\n       on lands, territories and waters traditionally occupied or used by indigenous and local communities.14\n       28. The Akwé: Kon Voluntary Guidelines remain valid and applicable to environmental impact\n       assessments, in particular in the context of the implementation of the Framework and Task 1.2 of the\n       programme of work.\n       29. The Akwé: Kon Voluntary Guidelines include cultural and social dimensions in environmental\n       impact assessments, including evaluating impacts on spiritual values, cultural practices, customary\n       governance systems, traditional livelihoods and intergenerational knowledge transmission.\n       30. Parties, other Governments, the private sector and organizations of indigenous peoples and\n       local communities may develop and apply other recognized, applicable and effective principles,\n       standards, agreements and protocols for environmental, cultural and social impact assessments.\n\n\n\n\n[12 Indicators for reporting on the goals and targets of the Framework are contained in decision 16/31 of 27 February 2025.]\n13 Decision VII/16 F, annex.\n14 The Conference of the Parties decided to use “indigenous peoples and local communities” in decision XII/12 and “prior and\n\ninformed consent”, “free, prior and informed consent” and “approval and involvement” depending on national circumstances, in\nthe Mo’otz Kuxtal Voluntary Guidelines in decision XIII/18.\n\n                                                                                                                        7/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"24. 原住民族與傳統領地的復育工作，在可能的情況下，應有助於糧食與水安全、氣候與社區韌性、收入創造，以及原住民族與地方社區之文化與生活方式強化。","html":"<p>24. 原住民族與傳統領地的復育工作，在可能的情況下，應有助於糧食與水安全、氣候與社區韌性、收入創造，以及原住民族與地方社區之文化與生活方式強化。</p>"},{"md":"VI. 在實施《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》目標 3 的過程中，對原住民族與傳統領地的承認與尊重\n25. 應制定、採納並實施用於國家層級識別、承認及尊重原住民族與傳統領地的法律、行政與政策措施。在適當情況下，該等措施應承認原住民族與地方社區之習慣土地權制度、治理機構、文化意義、聖地、保育角色、傳統經濟，以及對土地、水域、領地與資源之權利。\n26. 在許多情況下，原住民族與傳統領地對於生物多樣性的保育與永續利用有所貢獻，且與實施綱要目標 3 相關。原住民族與傳統領地亦可與保護區及其他保育共生地互補，且可能與其分開承認，[並進行報告][且考慮納入報告12]，並視實施綱要目標 3 之相關情況，根據自由、事先及知情同意原則以及國家情況、立法與框架而定。當保護區或其他保育共生地與原住民族及傳統領地重疊時，應根據國家立法、情況及自由、事先及知情同意原則，採取確保生物多樣性成果之管理措施，並尊重原住民族與地方社區對其傳統領地之權利，包括根據國家情況與立法，行使其習慣性永續利用、管理與治理以及文化實踐之權利。","html":"<p>VI. 在實施《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》目標 3 的過程中，對原住民族與傳統領地的承認與尊重<br />\n25. 應制定、採納並實施用於國家層級識別、承認及尊重原住民族與傳統領地的法律、行政與政策措施。在適當情況下，該等措施應承認原住民族與地方社區之習慣土地權制度、治理機構、文化意義、聖地、保育角色、傳統經濟，以及對土地、水域、領地與資源之權利。<br />\n26. 在許多情況下，原住民族與傳統領地對於生物多樣性的保育與永續利用有所貢獻，且與實施綱要目標 3 相關。原住民族與傳統領地亦可與保護區及其他保育共生地互補，且可能與其分開承認，[並進行報告][且考慮納入報告12]，並視實施綱要目標 3 之相關情況，根據自由、事先及知情同意原則以及國家情況、立法與框架而定。當保護區或其他保育共生地與原住民族及傳統領地重疊時，應根據國家立法、情況及自由、事先及知情同意原則，採取確保生物多樣性成果之管理措施，並尊重原住民族與地方社區對其傳統領地之權利，包括根據國家情況與立法，行使其習慣性永續利用、管理與治理以及文化實踐之權利。</p>"},{"md":"VII. 環境影響評估\n27. 《關於擬在聖地進行、或可能影響聖地以及由原住民族與地方社區傳統佔用或使用之土地與水域進行開發之文化、環境及社會影響評估之 Akwé: Kon 自願性指南》13，為針對擬在聖地或其附近，或在原住民族與地方社區傳統佔用或使用之土地、領地與水域進行開發，所進行之環境、文化及社會影響評估，提供了一個文化適切之框架。14\n28. 《Akwé: Kon 自願性指南》對於環境影響評估仍然有效且適用，特別是在實施綱要及工作計畫任務 1.2 的背景下。\n29. 《Akwé: Kon 自願性指南》在環境影響評估中納入了文化與社會維度，包括評估對精神價值、文化實踐、習慣治理制度、傳統生計及世代間知識傳承之影響。\n30. 締約方、其他政府、私營部門以及原住民族與地方社區組織，得針對環境、文化及社會影響評估，制定並應用其他經認可、適用且有效之原則、標準、協議與議定書。","html":"<p>VII. 環境影響評估<br />\n27. 《關於擬在聖地進行、或可能影響聖地以及由原住民族與地方社區傳統佔用或使用之土地與水域進行開發之文化、環境及社會影響評估之 Akwé: Kon 自願性指南》13，為針對擬在聖地或其附近，或在原住民族與地方社區傳統佔用或使用之土地、領地與水域進行開發，所進行之環境、文化及社會影響評估，提供了一個文化適切之框架。14<br />\n28. 《Akwé: Kon 自願性指南》對於環境影響評估仍然有效且適用，特別是在實施綱要及工作計畫任務 1.2 的背景下。<br />\n29. 《Akwé: Kon 自願性指南》在環境影響評估中納入了文化與社會維度，包括評估對精神價值、文化實踐、習慣治理制度、傳統生計及世代間知識傳承之影響。<br />\n30. 締約方、其他政府、私營部門以及原住民族與地方社區組織，得針對環境、文化及社會影響評估，制定並應用其他經認可、適用且有效之原則、標準、協議與議定書。</p>"},{"md":"[12 用於報告綱要目標與指標之指標，包含於 2025 年 2 月 27 日之決議 16/31 中。]\n13 決議 VII/16 F，附件。\n14 締約方大會在決議 XIII/18 之 Mo’otz Kuxtal 自願性指南中決定，視國家情況，在決議 XII/12 中使用「原住民族與地方社區」，並使用「事先及知情同意」、「自由、事先及知情同意」以及「核准與參與」。","html":"<p>[12 用於報告綱要目標與指標之指標，包含於 2025 年 2 月 27 日之決議 16/31 中。]<br />\n13 決議 VII/16 F，附件。<br />\n14 締約方大會在決議 XIII/18 之 Mo’otz Kuxtal 自願性指南中決定，視國家情況，在決議 XII/12 中使用「原住民族與地方社區」，並使用「事先及知情同意」、「自由、事先及知情同意」以及「核准與參與」。</p>"}]},{"p":"p008","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p008.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n       VIII. Institutional and administrative mechanisms\n       A.    Recognition of customary governance systems\n       31. The governance systems of indigenous peoples and local communities, including traditional\n       and customary governance systems, should be recognized and supported as unique and legitimate\n       frameworks for the stewardship, conservation and restoration of biodiversity, in accordance with\n       national legislation and circumstances, as well as international law and instruments. This recognition\n       should extend to customary laws, community protocols, norms, values and rules related to land, water\n       and marine management, as well as the protection of sacred sites.\n       32. Enabling conditions should be created to ensure support for indigenous peoples and local\n       communities in strengthening and exercising their governance systems for stewardship, conservation\n       and restoration of biodiversity, for example, through the recognition of and support for community\n       mapping, the participatory demarcation of their territories and the development of community-led\n       monitoring systems, in accordance with national legislation and circumstances.\n       33. Where applicable, the autonomy of indigenous peoples and local communities in the\n       management and governance of their lands, territories and waters, including marine and coastal areas,\n       should be respected through the recognition of their justice and governance systems and the provision\n       of financial and technical support.\n       34. Strengthening the customary governance systems of indigenous peoples and local\n       communities, in line with national legislation and circumstances, where applicable, for stewardship,\n       conservation and restoration of biodiversity supports more effective spatial planning and sustainable\n       management.\n       B.    Free, prior and informed consent\n       35. Mechanisms, legislation, administrative, corporate and policy measures or protocols should be\n       developed and implemented in consultation with indigenous peoples and local communities, as\n       appropriate and in accordance with national legislation and circumstances and international\n       instruments, with a view to obtaining their free, prior and informed consent before the establishment,\n       expansion or change of any protected area or any other effective area-based conservation measures\n       affecting their lands, territories and waters, including marine and coastal areas, or their cultural values\n       and heritage. Free, prior and informed consent should be obtained through consultations conducted\n       in good faith as part of environmental impact assessments and in accordance with national legislation\n       and the Akwé: Kon Voluntary Guidelines.\n       36. Free, prior and informed consent should be understood as an ongoing process, allowing\n       communities to determine what information should be shared or withheld, in particular with regard\n       to sacred sites and culturally sensitive information.\n       37. Traditional knowledge, innovations and practices should only be accessed and used with the\n       free, prior and informed consent of indigenous peoples and local communities, in line with customary\n       protocols, national legislation and international instruments.\n       C.    Administrative arrangements\n       38. Indigenous and traditional mechanisms, including relevant governance frameworks, in\n       accordance with indigenous and traditional governance systems should be established or, as needed,\n       strengthened or maintained.\n       39. Co-management bodies should be established or strengthened under national legislation, as\n       appropriate, to guide the planning, implementation and monitoring of conservation and ecosystem\n       restoration efforts, spatial planning and environmental impact assessments. Co-management bodies\n       should ensure an equitable representation of government and indigenous peoples and local\n       communities.\n\n\n8/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"VIII. 機制與行政機制\nA.","html":"<p>VIII. 機制與行政機制<br />\nA.</p>"},{"md":"慣習治理制度之承認","html":"<p>慣習治理制度之承認</p>"},{"md":"31. 原住民族與地方社區之治理制度，包括傳統與慣習治理制度，應在符合國家立法與情況以及國際法與文書之前提下，被承認並支持其作為生物多樣性管理、保育與復育之獨特且合法框架。此項承認應延伸至與土地、水資源及海洋管理，以及聖地保護相關之慣習法、社區議定、規範、價值觀與規則。\n32. 應創造有利條件，以確保支持原住民族與地方社區強化並行使其用於生物多樣性管理、保育與復育之治理制度，例如，在符合國家立法與情況之前提下，透過承認並支持社區地圖繪製、其領土之參與式劃界，以及開發由社區主導之監測系統。\n33. 在適用情況下，應透過承認其司法與治理制度，並提供財務與技術支持，以尊重原住民族與地方社區在其土地、領土與水域（包括海洋與沿海區域）管理與治理方面之自主權。\n34. 在適用情況下，根據國家立法與情況，強化原住民族與地方社區用於生物多樣性管理、保育與復育之慣習治理制度，有助於更有效地進行空間規劃與永續管理。","html":"<p>31. 原住民族與地方社區之治理制度，包括傳統與慣習治理制度，應在符合國家立法與情況以及國際法與文書之前提下，被承認並支持其作為生物多樣性管理、保育與復育之獨特且合法框架。此項承認應延伸至與土地、水資源及海洋管理，以及聖地保護相關之慣習法、社區議定、規範、價值觀與規則。<br />\n32. 應創造有利條件，以確保支持原住民族與地方社區強化並行使其用於生物多樣性管理、保育與復育之治理制度，例如，在符合國家立法與情況之前提下，透過承認並支持社區地圖繪製、其領土之參與式劃界，以及開發由社區主導之監測系統。<br />\n33. 在適用情況下，應透過承認其司法與治理制度，並提供財務與技術支持，以尊重原住民族與地方社區在其土地、領土與水域（包括海洋與沿海區域）管理與治理方面之自主權。<br />\n34. 在適用情況下，根據國家立法與情況，強化原住民族與地方社區用於生物多樣性管理、保育與復育之慣習治理制度，有助於更有效地進行空間規劃與永續管理。</p>"},{"md":"B.","html":"<p>B.</p>"},{"md":"自由、事先與知情同意","html":"<p>自由、事先與知情同意</p>","pre":"效地進行空間規劃與永續管理。\n\nB.\n\n"},{"md":"35. 應透過與原住民族與地方社區諮商，並在適當且符合國家立法、情況與國際文書之前提下，制定並實施機制、立法、行政、企業與政策措施或議定，旨在於任何影響其土地、領土與水域（包括海洋與沿海區域）或其文化價值與遺產之保護區或任何其他保育共生地之設立、擴張或變更前，取得其自由、事先與知情同意。自由、事先與知情同意應透過作為環境影響評估之一環，且符合國家立法與 Akwé: Kon 自願性指南之誠信諮商來取得。\n36. 自由、事先與知情同意應被理解為一個持續性的過程，允許社區決定應分享或保留哪些資訊，特別是關於聖地與文化敏感資訊之部分。\n37. 傳統知識、創新與實務，僅應在符合慣習議定、國家立法與國際文書之前提下，取得原住民族與地方社區之自由、事先與知情同意後，方可進行取得與使用。","html":"<p>35. 應透過與原住民族與地方社區諮商，並在適當且符合國家立法、情況與國際文書之前提下，制定並實施機制、立法、行政、企業與政策措施或議定，旨在於任何影響其土地、領土與水域（包括海洋與沿海區域）或其文化價值與遺產之保護區或任何其他保育共生地之設立、擴張或變更前，取得其自由、事先與知情同意。自由、事先與知情同意應透過作為環境影響評估之一環，且符合國家立法與 Akwé: Kon 自願性指南之誠信諮商來取得。<br />\n36. 自由、事先與知情同意應被理解為一個持續性的過程，允許社區決定應分享或保留哪些資訊，特別是關於聖地與文化敏感資訊之部分。<br />\n37. 傳統知識、創新與實務，僅應在符合慣習議定、國家立法與國際文書之前提下，取得原住民族與地方社區之自由、事先與知情同意後，方可進行取得與使用。</p>"},{"md":"C.","html":"<p>C.</p>"},{"md":"行政安排","html":"<p>行政安排</p>"},{"md":"38. 應建立或在必要時強化或維持符合原住民族與傳統治理制度之原住民與傳統機制，包括相關之治理框架。\n39. 應在適當情況下，根據國家立法建立或強化共同管理機構，以指導保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估之規劃、實施與監測。共同管理機構應確保政府、原住民族與地方社區之公平代表性。","html":"<p>38. 應建立或在必要時強化或維持符合原住民族與傳統治理制度之原住民與傳統機制，包括相關之治理框架。<br />\n39. 應在適當情況下，根據國家立法建立或強化共同管理機構，以指導保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估之規劃、實施與監測。共同管理機構應確保政府、原住民族與地方社區之公平代表性。</p>"}]},{"p":"p009","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p009.md","en":"                                                                                     CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n40. Areas that are governed and managed by indigenous peoples and local communities in\naccordance with their customary laws and practices, governance systems and institutional\nframeworks should be recognized in the context of conservation and ecosystem restoration efforts,\nspatial planning and environmental impact assessments, in line with national legislation and\ncircumstances, and international instruments.\n41. The customary law, procedures and governance systems of indigenous peoples and local\ncommunities may be recognized and respected in the design, management and monitoring of\nprotected areas, as appropriate. Such recognition may also be reflected in other effective area-based\nconservation measures, spatial planning and environmental impact assessments.\n42. Consultative and collaborative mechanisms should be established or, as needed, strengthened\nbetween governments and representative bodies and organizations of indigenous peoples and local\ncommunities, as determined by them, in their respective countries, in relation to community mapping\ninitiatives and monitoring systems.\n43. Mechanisms, legislation, administrative, corporate and policy measures, including safeguards,\ngrievance mechanisms and monitoring systems, should be developed and implemented to prevent\nmarginalization, displacement or cultural harm in connection with conservation, restoration and\nsustainable use efforts, spatial planning and environmental impact assessments.\nD.    Mechanisms for conflict resolution\n44. Mechanisms, legislation, administrative, corporate and policy measures or protocols for\nconflict prevention, resolution and grievance redress should be established or, as needed,\nstrengthened. Those systems should recognize and respect customary dispute resolution practices\nand safeguard the rights of indigenous peoples and local communities in relation to protected areas\nand other effective area-based conservation measures, and ecosystem restoration efforts, spatial\nplanning and environmental impact assessments.\n\nIX. Full and effective participation\n45. Indigenous peoples and local communities should be accorded the right to full, equitable,\ninclusive, effective and gender-responsive participation in all decision-making processes that may\naffect their rights, lands, territories, waters and resources.\n46. The participation of indigenous peoples and local communities should be ensured throughout\nthe development, planning, implementation and monitoring of protected areas and other effective\narea-based conservation and ecosystem restoration measures, spatial planning and environmental\nimpact assessments that affect them.\n\nX.    Enabling conditions\nA.    Capacity-building\n47. The capacity of indigenous peoples and local communities, relevant government institutions\nand the private sector to implement the present guidelines should be strengthened. Capacity-building\nefforts should be culturally sensitive and include training for public authorities, stakeholders and the\nprivate sector to apply relevant legislation and policies respectfully and effectively.\n48. Opportunities for training, technical cooperation and legal support, including, where possible,\nin indigenous languages, should be created and promoted through the development of appropriate\nmaterials and tools to allow for the sharing of experiences related to protected areas and other\neffective area-based conservation and ecosystem restoration efforts, spatial planning and\nenvironmental impact assessments among indigenous peoples and local communities.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                   9/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"40. 在符合國家立法、現況及國際文書的情況下，應在保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估的脈絡下，承認由原住民族與地方社區依其慣習法與慣行、治理系統及制度框架所管理與經營之區域。\n41. 在適當情況下，原住民族與地方社區的慣習法、程序與治理系統，可在保護區的設計、管理與監測中獲得承認與尊重。此類承認亦可反映於其他保育共生地、空間規劃及環境影響評估中。\n42. 應在各國政府與由其自行決定之原住民族與地方社區代表機構及組織之間，針對社區地圖繪製倡議與監測系統，建立或於必要時強化諮詢與協作機制。\n43. 應制定並落實機制、立法、行政、企業與政策措施，包括安全保障、申訴機制與監測系統，以防止與保育、復育與永續利用工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估相關之邊緣化、流離失所或文化傷害。","html":"<p>40. 在符合國家立法、現況及國際文書的情況下，應在保育與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估的脈絡下，承認由原住民族與地方社區依其慣習法與慣行、治理系統及制度框架所管理與經營之區域。<br />\n41. 在適當情況下，原住民族與地方社區的慣習法、程序與治理系統，可在保護區的設計、管理與監測中獲得承認與尊重。此類承認亦可反映於其他保育共生地、空間規劃及環境影響評估中。<br />\n42. 應在各國政府與由其自行決定之原住民族與地方社區代表機構及組織之間，針對社區地圖繪製倡議與監測系統，建立或於必要時強化諮詢與協作機制。<br />\n43. 應制定並落實機制、立法、行政、企業與政策措施，包括安全保障、申訴機制與監測系統，以防止與保育、復育與永續利用工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估相關之邊緣化、流離失所或文化傷害。</p>"},{"md":"D. 衝突解決機制","html":"<p>D. 衝突解決機制</p>"},{"md":"44. 應建立或於必要時強化用於衝突預防、解決與申訴救濟的機制、立法、行政、企業與政策措施或議定書。該等系統應承認並尊重慣習性爭議解決慣行，並保障原住民族與地方社區在涉及保護區、其他保育共生地、生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估方面的權利。","html":"<p>44. 應建立或於必要時強化用於衝突預防、解決與申訴救濟的機制、立法、行政、企業與政策措施或議定書。該等系統應承認並尊重慣習性爭議解決慣行，並保障原住民族與地方社區在涉及保護區、其他保育共生地、生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估方面的權利。</p>"},{"md":"IX. 全面且有效的參與","html":"<p>IX. 全面且有效的參與</p>"},{"md":"45. 原住民族與地方社區應被賦予權利，在所有可能影響其權利、土地、領土、水域與資源的決策過程中，享有全面、公平、包容、有效且具性別回應性的參與。\n46. 應確保原住民族與地方社區在涉及其利益的保護區、其他保育共生地與生態系復育措施、空間規劃及環境影響評估的開發、規劃、執行與監測過程中皆能參與。","html":"<p>45. 原住民族與地方社區應被賦予權利，在所有可能影響其權利、土地、領土、水域與資源的決策過程中，享有全面、公平、包容、有效且具性別回應性的參與。<br />\n46. 應確保原住民族與地方社區在涉及其利益的保護區、其他保育共生地與生態系復育措施、空間規劃及環境影響評估的開發、規劃、執行與監測過程中皆能參與。</p>"},{"md":"X. 促成條件","html":"<p>X. 促成條件</p>"},{"md":"A. 能力建構","html":"<p>A. 能力建構</p>"},{"md":"47. 應強化原住民族與地方社區、相關政府機構及私營部門執行本指南之能力。能力建構工作應具備文化敏感性，並包括為公務機關、利害關係人及私營部門提供培訓，以使其能尊重且有效地應用相關立法與政策。\n48. 應透過開發適當的教材與工具，創造並促進培訓、技術合作與法律支援之機會（在可能情況下包括使用原住民族語言），以利原住民族與地方社區之間分享與保護區、其他保育共生地與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估相關之經驗。","html":"<p>47. 應強化原住民族與地方社區、相關政府機構及私營部門執行本指南之能力。能力建構工作應具備文化敏感性，並包括為公務機關、利害關係人及私營部門提供培訓，以使其能尊重且有效地應用相關立法與政策。<br />\n48. 應透過開發適當的教材與工具，創造並促進培訓、技術合作與法律支援之機會（在可能情況下包括使用原住民族語言），以利原住民族與地方社區之間分享與保護區、其他保育共生地與生態系復育工作、空間規劃及環境影響評估相關之經驗。</p>"}]},{"p":"p010","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p010.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\n        B.     Awareness-raising\n        49. Awareness should be raised among government institutions, civil society, the private sector\n        and the general public of the roles, contributions, rights and knowledge systems of indigenous\n        peoples and local communities in conservation and ecosystem restoration.\n        C.     Sustainable, inclusive and accessible financial mechanisms\n        50. Adequate sustainable and accessible financial support should be provided or strengthened for\n        the design, implementation and monitoring of protected areas and other effective area-based\n        conservation measures, ecosystem restoration measures, spatial planning and environmental impact\n        assessments led by or involving indigenous peoples and local communities, in line with\n        decisions 16/4 and 16/34.\n        51. Direct funding programmes should be developed, established or strengthened, as appropriate,\n        in coordination with Parties, consistent with national legislation and circumstances, to support\n        restoration and conservation efforts, spatial planning processes and environmental impact\n        assessments led by indigenous peoples and local communities within their lands, territories and\n        waters with their full and effective participation, in line with decisions 16/4 and 16/34.\n        D.     Monitoring and reporting\n        52. The development of monitoring and reporting mechanisms, including the use of biocultural\n        community protocols, should be supported.\n        53. Quantitative and qualitative indicators, including biocultural and traditional knowledge\n        indicators, and community-based monitoring and information systems should be promoted,\n        strengthened and, where appropriate, operationalized, taking into account national circumstances,\n        without creating additional reporting burdens, to measure the effectiveness of conservation and\n        ecosystem restoration efforts led by or involving indigenous peoples and local communities,\n        including for headline indicator 22.1 of the monitoring framework for the Kunming-Montreal Global\n        Biodiversity Framework on land-use change and land tenure in the traditional territories of\n        indigenous peoples and local communities.15\n        54. The need to ensure and enhance the transparency and inclusivity of community-based\n        monitoring and information systems and to fully engage indigenous peoples and local communities,\n        including women and youth among them, in the management of those systems, including by\n        promoting and implementing community-based monitoring and information systems in developing\n        countries, should be acknowledged, and the importance of enhanced international cooperation to\n        address the technical, financial, technological and capacity constraints that undermine the capacity\n        of developing countries to support community-based monitoring and information systems should be\n        highlighted.\n        55. National information systems on indigenous and traditional territories, at all levels, should be\n        created and maintained, in accordance with national circumstances, legislation and priorities, to\n        monitor progress in the recognition and governance of those territories. The systems must ensure\n        data sovereignty for those groups, with their free, prior and informed consent.\n        56. Information on the implementation of commitments related to indigenous and traditional\n        territories, and the contributions of indigenous peoples and local communities to conservation and\n        ecosystem restoration, should be integrated into national biodiversity strategies and action plans,\n        national restoration plans and national reports produced under the Convention.\n        E.     Access to information\n        57. Indigenous peoples and local communities should have timely and culturally appropriate\n        access to information related to protected areas and other effective area-based conservation measures,\n\n15 See decision 16/31.\n\n\n\n10/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"B.","html":"<p>B.</p>"},{"md":"提升意識","html":"<p>提升意識</p>"},{"md":"49. 應提升政府機構、公民社會、私營部門及大眾對於原住民族與地方社區在保育與生態系復育中所扮演的角色、貢獻、權利及知識系統之認識。","html":"<p>49. 應提升政府機構、公民社會、私營部門及大眾對於原住民族與地方社區在保育與生態系復育中所扮演的角色、貢獻、權利及知識系統之認識。</p>"},{"md":"C.","html":"<p>C.</p>"},{"md":"永續、包容且可取得之財務機制","html":"<p>永續、包容且可取得之財務機制</p>"},{"md":"50. 應根據決議 16/4 與 16/34，針對由原住民族與地方社區主導或涉及其參與之保護區與保育共生地、生態系復育措施、空間規劃及環境影響評估的設計、實施與監測，提供或強化充足、永續且可取得之財務支持。\n51. 應視情況與締約方協調，在符合國家立法與情況下，開發、建立或強化直接資助計畫，以支持由原住民族與地方社區在其土地、領土與水域內，透過其充分且有效之參與，所主導之復育與保育工作、空間規劃程序及環境影響評估，並符合決議 16/4 與 16/34。","html":"<p>50. 應根據決議 16/4 與 16/34，針對由原住民族與地方社區主導或涉及其參與之保護區與保育共生地、生態系復育措施、空間規劃及環境影響評估的設計、實施與監測，提供或強化充足、永續且可取得之財務支持。<br />\n51. 應視情況與締約方協調，在符合國家立法與情況下，開發、建立或強化直接資助計畫，以支持由原住民族與地方社區在其土地、領土與水域內，透過其充分且有效之參與，所主導之復育與保育工作、空間規劃程序及環境影響評估，並符合決議 16/4 與 16/34。</p>"},{"md":"D.","html":"<p>D.</p>"},{"md":"監測與報告","html":"<p>監測與報告</p>","pre":" 16/4 與 16/34。\n\nD.\n\n"},{"md":"52. 應支持監測與報告機制之開發，包括生物文化社區協定之使用。\n53. 應促進、強化並在適當情況下落實量化與質性指標（包括生物文化與傳統知識指標）以及以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統，並考量國家情況，在不增加額外報告負擔的前提下，用以衡量由原住民族與地方社區主導或涉及其參與之保育與生態系復育工作成效，包括用於昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要監測架構中，關於原住民族與地方社區傳統領土內土地利用變化與土地權屬之主要指標 22.1。¹⁵\n54. 應承認有必要確保並增強以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統的透明度與包容性，並讓原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）充分參與該等系統的管理，包括透過在開發中國家促進與落實以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統；且應強調強化國際合作之重要性，以應對削弱開發中國家支持以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統能力之技術、財務、技術及能力限制。\n55. 應根據國家情況、立法與優先事項，建立並維護各層級關於原住民族與傳統領土之國家資訊系統，以監測該等領土在承認與治理方面之進展。該系統必須在取得該族群自由、事先及知情同意的前提下，確保其資料主權。\n56. 有關原住民族與傳統領土相關承諾之履行資訊，以及原住民族與地方社區對保育與生態系復育之貢獻，應整合至國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家復育計畫以及根據本公約所編製之國家報告中。","html":"<p>52. 應支持監測與報告機制之開發，包括生物文化社區協定之使用。<br />\n53. 應促進、強化並在適當情況下落實量化與質性指標（包括生物文化與傳統知識指標）以及以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統，並考量國家情況，在不增加額外報告負擔的前提下，用以衡量由原住民族與地方社區主導或涉及其參與之保育與生態系復育工作成效，包括用於昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要監測架構中，關於原住民族與地方社區傳統領土內土地利用變化與土地權屬之主要指標 22.1。¹⁵<br />\n54. 應承認有必要確保並增強以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統的透明度與包容性，並讓原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）充分參與該等系統的管理，包括透過在開發中國家促進與落實以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統；且應強調強化國際合作之重要性，以應對削弱開發中國家支持以社區為基礎之監測與資訊系統能力之技術、財務、技術及能力限制。<br />\n55. 應根據國家情況、立法與優先事項，建立並維護各層級關於原住民族與傳統領土之國家資訊系統，以監測該等領土在承認與治理方面之進展。該系統必須在取得該族群自由、事先及知情同意的前提下，確保其資料主權。<br />\n56. 有關原住民族與傳統領土相關承諾之履行資訊，以及原住民族與地方社區對保育與生態系復育之貢獻，應整合至國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家復育計畫以及根據本公約所編製之國家報告中。</p>"},{"md":"E.","html":"<p>E.</p>"},{"md":"取得資訊","html":"<p>取得資訊</p>"},{"md":"57. 原住民族與地方社區對於與保護區及保育共生地相關之資訊，應具有及時且符合文化適切性之取得權。","html":"<p>57. 原住民族與地方社區對於與保護區及保育共生地相關之資訊，應具有及時且符合文化適切性之取得權。</p>"},{"md":"***","html":"<hr />"},{"md":"¹⁵ 見決議 16/31。","html":"<p>¹⁵ 見決議 16/31。</p>"}]},{"p":"p011","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae4c6f3496641e2d89dffd3/p011.md","en":"                                                                                    CBD/COP/17/L.28\n\necosystem restoration, spatial planning and environmental impact assessments. Access includes the\ntranslation of relevant materials into the languages of indigenous peoples and local communities, as\nappropriate, as well as the use of culturally appropriate formats to facilitate the full and effective\nparticipation of indigenous peoples and local communities, including women and youth among them.\nF.    Technical cooperation and legal support\n58. Technical, scientific and legal support should be provided to strengthen the capacity of\nindigenous peoples and local communities to undertake community mapping and monitoring related\nto protected areas and other effective area-based conservation measures, ecosystem restoration,\nspatial planning and environmental impact assessments.\n59. Legal support should be provided, as appropriate, in line with national circumstances, to\nenhance awareness of the collective land rights of indigenous peoples and local communities,\nincluding women and youth among them, and their right to a traditional lifestyle and to assist\nindigenous peoples and local communities with processes related to protected areas and other\neffective area-based conservation measures, ecosystem restoration, spatial planning and\nenvironmental impact assessments.\n                                            __________\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                11/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/L.28","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/L.28</p>"},{"md":"生態系復育、空間規劃與環境影響評估。取得權利包括在適當情況下將相關資料翻譯成原住民族與地方社區之語言，並使用符合文化適切性的格式，以促進原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）之全面且有效參與。","html":"<p>生態系復育、空間規劃與環境影響評估。取得權利包括在適當情況下將相關資料翻譯成原住民族與地方社區之語言，並使用符合文化適切性的格式，以促進原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）之全面且有效參與。</p>"},{"md":"F.","html":"<p>F.</p>"},{"md":"技術合作與法律支援","html":"<p>技術合作與法律支援</p>"},{"md":"58. 應提供技術、科學與法律支援，以強化原住民族與地方社區進行與保護區及其他保育共生地、生態系復育、空間規劃與環境影響評估相關之社區繪圖與監測之能力。","html":"<p>58. 應提供技術、科學與法律支援，以強化原住民族與地方社區進行與保護區及其他保育共生地、生態系復育、空間規劃與環境影響評估相關之社區繪圖與監測之能力。</p>"},{"md":"59. 應根據國家情況，在適當情況下提供法律支援，以增進對於原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）集體土地權利及其傳統生活方式權利之意識，並協助原住民族與地方社區處理與保護區及其他保育共生地、生態系復育、空間規劃與環境影響評估相關之程序。","html":"<p>59. 應根據國家情況，在適當情況下提供法律支援，以增進對於原住民族與地方社區（包括其中的女性與青年）集體土地權利及其傳統生活方式權利之意識，並協助原住民族與地方社區處理與保護區及其他保育共生地、生態系復育、空間規劃與環境影響評估相關之程序。</p>"},{"md":"__________","html":"<hr />"},{"md":"11/11","html":"<p>11/11</p>"}]}]};
